    "reading-passage": {
      "nodeId": "542:1373",
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "📖 Reading Passage",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "📖 Reading Passage"
        },
        {
          "text": "Level: B1 · Topic: The Kitchen",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "Level: B1 · Topic: The Kitchen"
        },
        {
          "text": "Cooking at Home\n\nMy kitchen is my favourite room in the house. Every evening after work, I spend about an hour there preparing dinner. The kitchen has all the appliances I need: a large refrigerator to keep food fresh, a stove with four burners, and an oven for baking. I also use the microwave to heat leftovers quickly.\n\nOn the counter, I keep my most-used small appliances — a kettle for tea, a toaster for breakfast, a coffee maker, and a blender for smoothies. The dishwasher saves me a lot of time after meals, so I rarely wash dishes by hand anymore.\n\nI have a full set of cooking tools. My favourite pan is a non-stick frying pan I use almost every day. For soups and sauces, I reach for a large saucepan or a pot. When I make stir-fry, I use the wok. The pressure cooker is perfect for beans and stews because it cooks everything much faster.\n\nIn the drawer next to the stove, I keep my utensils: a spatula for flipping, a ladle for serving soup, tongs for grilling, and a whisk for eggs and batters. The peeler and grater sit in a small container beside the cutting board, along with a sharp knife.\n\nI store dry food in the pantry and fresh items on the shelves inside the refrigerator. The freezer is full of frozen vegetables and ice cream. Cooking at home is cheaper, healthier, and honestly, more relaxing than eating out.",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "Cooking at Home My kitchen is my favourite room in the house. Every evening after work, I spend about an hour there preparing dinner. The kitchen has all the appliances I need: a large refrigerator to keep food fresh, a stove with four burners, and an oven for baking. I also use the microwave to heat leftovers quickly. On the counter, I keep my most-used small appliances — a kettle for tea, a toaster for breakfast, a coffee maker, and a blender for smoothies. The dishwasher saves me a lot of time after meals, so I rarely wash dishes by hand anymore. I have a full set of cooking tools. My favourite pan is a non-stick frying pan I use almost every day. For soups and sauces, I reach for a large saucepan or a pot. When I make stir-fry, I use the wok. The pressure cooker is perfect for beans and stews because it cooks everything much faster. In the drawer next to the stove, I keep my utensils: a spatula for flipping, a ladle for serving soup, tongs for grilling, and a whisk for eggs and batters. The peeler and grater sit in a small container beside the cutting board, along with a sharp knife. I store dry food in the pantry and fresh items on the shelves inside the refrigerator. The freezer is full of frozen vegetables and ice cream. Cooking at home is cheaper, healthier, and honestly, more relaxing than eating out."
        },
        {
          "text": "Comprehension Questions",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "Comprehension Questions"
        },
        {
          "text": "1. How long does the writer spend cooking each evening?",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "1. How long does the writer spend cooking each evening?"
        },
        {
          "text": "2. Which appliance does the writer use to heat leftovers?",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "2. Which appliance does the writer use to heat leftovers?"
        },
        {
          "text": "3. What does the writer use the wok for?",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "3. What does the writer use the wok for?"
        },
        {
          "text": "4. Where does the writer store dry food?",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "4. Where does the writer store dry food?"
        },
        {
          "text": "5. Why does the writer prefer cooking at home?",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "5. Why does the writer prefer cooking at home?"
        }
      ]
    },
    "idioms-phrases": {
      "nodeId": "542:1386",
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "💬 Idioms, Phrasal Verbs & Key Phrases",
          "depth": 1,
          "name": "💬 Idioms, Phrasal Verbs & Key Phrases"
        },
        {
          "text": "too many cooks spoil the broth",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "too many cooks spoil the broth"
        },
        {
          "text": "too many people trying to do the same thing make the result worse",
          "depth": 2,
          "name": "too many people trying to do the same thing make the result worse"
        },
        {
          "text": "\"Let me handle dinner — too many cooks spoil the broth.\"",
          "depth": 2,
